# 🗂️ Verification evidence: the conclusion is published, the process is not.
#
# Every run under benchmarks/results/ used to be committed whole — raw tool
# output, config copies, environment dumps. That was thousands of files nobody
# reads, and it is where incidental personal detail kept turning up (an ACME
# account address, EC2 instance ids). It was also already broken: the `*.log`
# rule above silently dropped exactly the log a RESULT.md cited as proof, so
# the repository advertised an audit trail it did not contain.
#
# What stays: the summary (RESULT.md / README.md / result.txt), the integrity
# ledger (SHA256SUMS), and the commit under test (commit.txt). SHA256SUMS is
# what makes this safe — a privately archived raw tree can still be verified
# byte-for-byte against the published hashes.
benchmarks/results/**
!benchmarks/results/*/
!benchmarks/results/**/RESULT.md
!benchmarks/results/**/README.md
!benchmarks/results/**/SHA256SUMS
!benchmarks/results/**/commit.txt
!benchmarks/results/**/result.txt

# 📋 Planning documents are working material, kept local.
#
# TODO.md tracks known-but-unfixed weaknesses day by day, including
# reproducible ones in already-released code. A public, prioritised list of
# your own unpatched defects is a target list; publish the fix, not the queue.
#
# SITE.md is the website plan. Same shelf for the same reason plans belong
# there: they contradict themselves as facts arrive, and a plan read as a
# promise is worse than no plan. Publish the site, not the sketch of it.
docs/TODO.md
docs/SITE.md
docs/TODO_CADDYFILE*.md

